y ways they're  an ecological desert much of what should  be here isn't here anymore but there are  remnants there are patches of the  original caledonian forest beautiful  peatlands that can be restored and  nurtured and be the sort of source of a  wider landscape change they're coming  around now  but not everyone's convinced angus  mcfadden has 1300 ewes several dozen  cows and he says plenty of wildlife on  his farm he doesn't see the sense in  covering good land with trees  these hills that i'm farming on because  of legislation that's there now couldn't  take trees i'm talking about things like  the depth of peat the steepness of the  banks  we couldn't plant cheese on there so  better a good moodle management scheme  proper grazing regime  to help store the carbon  at bun loyt they're hoping to show  rewilding farming and forestry can  co-exist but it'll take generations and  funding to replant the highlands and use  scotland's landscape to hold back the  climate crisis  thomas more sky news loch ness  well time now to discuss all the climate  issues of the day and the developments  here at cop with john verdick who's  director of the international climate  policy at the nature conservancy so  welcome to you  so we're just 24 hours off the scheduled  end of cop26 so how far away from a deal  are we and what are the key sticking  points that still remain i think we're  close um although close in cop time  means maybe two more days um cops tend  to go over a lot this is quite normal in  this process  the key sticking points there's a big  negotiation going on and what is how do  we make carbon markets work how do we  build up climate finance and then also  what is transparency um  paris the paris agreement is really  important based on transparency so we're  finishing up the rules on how do you  report on your greenhouse gas emissions  okay and and as far as that is concerned  there's been a lot of talk of countries  coming back in just one year i mean  originally under paris they were it was  every five years wasn't it so the idea  that people come back more frequently  and are put under pressure to improve  their targets is is that going to happen  is that is that what is likely to be in  the final text i i don't yeah i mean  there's some discussion of that being in  text i think actually paris said ten  years or five years because it didn't  say so you saw china come with a 10-year  commitment in paris united states come  with five they're both right because  neither was wrong now we've actually  built out that guidance a little bit in  the last years it said it was five  there's talk of one but you know just  like in many of our organizations where  we work if you do a reorganization every  year at some point you have to do the  work on the ground so sometimes doing a  new plan every year doesn't work for  some countries especially if you're  democratic and you change in one year  you might have a new leader that's going  to be more robust and what about the  finance is this all about  enough money for developing countries to  to transition away from fossil fuels or  is there an argument about how the money  spent whether more of that money should  go towards adaptation for example i  think there's two sides of the coin one  side is the remission reductions the  other side of the coin is how do you get  the emission reductions and if you're a  developing country how you get those  emissions reductions is the climate  finance many of the developing countries  also are not large emitters so they're  trying to think of what can they do and  what does climate change mean to them  and it means something different than  what it does to you know the eu  countries to the uk to united states um  but very important to them is figuring  out still how do they feed their  families how do they provide energy  well yes and as far as money is  concerned what needs to be agreed here  is it more money forthcoming or a  different way of